{
  "title": "Why Vision Never Blurs or Goes Dark When Looking Around | So That's Why Office",
  "description": "Human gaze jumps several times a second. As eyes dart rapidly, visual scenes sweep violently across the retina, and the lens wobbles like jelly upon sudden stops. If a handheld camera shook like that, footage would be completely blurred. Moreover, we blink a dozen to twenty times a minute, blacking out for over a hundred milliseconds each time. Why does our visual world never blur or shake, and why do we never sense the darkness? Where did that lost footage go?\n\nIn this episode, Chief and Kid uncover millions of years of seamless visual editing between the eyes and the brain: from isolated retina experiments at the University of Tübingen and attentional resets via blinks at NTU Singapore, all the way to eye-tracking telemetry in Formula race car drivers at 300 km/h!\n\nIllustrations generated with AI. All scientific facts and experimental data are verified against first-hand peer-reviewed research papers listed in the references below.\n\nInformation current as of 2026-11-17.",
  "tags": [
    "Saccade",
    "Blinking",
    "Saccadic Suppression",
    "Retina",
    "Brain Science",
    "Human Body",
    "So That's Why Office",
    "Science"
  ],
  "thumbnail": {
    "headline": "**1 Hour** Blind Daily?",
    "sub": "10,000 Blinks, Yet Zero Blackout!"
  },
  "chapters": {
    "mirror-look": "Every eye movement blurs and wobbles first",
    "dimmer-switch-concept": "Old theory: brain dims vision before eye turns",
    "kid-firm-guess": "First twist: isolated retina blinds itself",
    "backward-masking-strip": "The blurred slice: masked, habituated, and reused",
    "kid-frame-face": "Second twist: blink blackout sharpens vision",
    "chief-clapboard": "Brain the director: blinks at perfect moments",
    "kid-joyful-summary": "So that's why: eyes are adaptive smart systems"
  },
  "source_hashes": {
    "chapters": {
